{:edb/db-before-t 1812 :edb/db-after-t 1813 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535125 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:28:24.722Z" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128788 1100 "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/f3007b053a76b455c6c5b7ea617039780e6ee7b856ac2c54ae124dad334aef7f.png) express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/5c67d939121c919b3d51b562efbc774a6f2a702d2a8e9cadee508446f9bd35d0.png)" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128788 1100 "Given that $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x^2 + (y+2)^2 \\leq 4\\right\\}$, express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D e^{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}A$" 13194139535125 false] [17592186128788 1145 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$. Expanding the region gives $$x^2+(y+2)^2\\le4\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad r^2+4r\\sin\\theta\\le0.$$ The disk lies below the origin, so $\\pi\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$ and $0\\le r\\le-4\\sin\\theta$. Since $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, $$\\iint_De^{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\,dA=\\int_\\pi^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{-4\\sin\\theta}re^r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$" 13194139535125 false] [17592186128788 1145 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/7f645c475debdf46d06a9ecbebb3f019a7a92ea0c6129e06b8b5f326e005f9f6.png)\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/c592260e19248a715187208d54cafc6f4ceac8f34c843f1b366134bc37fb5430.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/33d4430552670d83a2c303e8ab286d3391f5611450f4e4f0b6ad6ccb58049b0e.png) represents the disk of radius ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/f7b723906930985b61bc58989f341d6b2e1e1f70ff34946ce5f5f373e2b0f95a.png) centered at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/484ceb02ac6fd60fc3d18cfff327672d25f5b88cb1b1825b8c1e069f153b9545.png) \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/00dbdb2d8638277969db40d2ca7c43266b4f275fbeadee86f6ef37954661433b.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/b78de9de64536b59ded324af0c67d84d3d64802a8a8d1af3894abfc686e2ae53.png) in polar coordinates:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/185d596ce2824cf09bf7e5e7fd4cc432b75dfbe442c2e653564d93f347acf7ee.png) \n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/427fd471dcf18cc90c67eb53f8f20e484fa707110281ad49811bc6a4f2ed5968.png) cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/f560ba12327ee0ad0c69dbd656ab82ac974acf442e8a1642dcf597f340d7f6db.png)\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/427fd471dcf18cc90c67eb53f8f20e484fa707110281ad49811bc6a4f2ed5968.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/4302d6f15afc8636ab514cdce845f4bbe0171d743153ee6bdfbbe2b323bf4ac4.png) First of all, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/15012c54ccf6548b6c3e2201e44d9ccfd33d7d41c627a0ce22c56f4c6d41d16c.png) can vary from ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/4efe6d0d575bd5dd59ca5e6e05092a1d2030164be2ce65efd468d385e931fddb.png) (at the origin) to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/cecc5f8feb56bb745748bbc79f2bfdc884364e7b9dce2c027c8c251366bb6f32.png) (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/f5ff820a0def58e4a2e6b15b5b77368151a71a72a083ef92fd95c20dacf711d3.png)\n\nSecondly, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/e10bbbc0bc5b9cc38adb59e4daa0cd709a3d7165da31914f73e17bf28cc01902.png) can vary between ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/a55c0db211022b11f154d509c19b94022e218e9bf3120144dc2464bf5ca5c29c.png) (the negative ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/245bebfeb6cb95babe872c0185e33d598fb9dd08c8950252c07cdb5f5c8f87d4.png)-axis) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/bc5b0c56a73784c1d673cae720a8d7796922204eb07796b954ff10b197fea2c1.png) (the positive ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/245bebfeb6cb95babe872c0185e33d598fb9dd08c8950252c07cdb5f5c8f87d4.png)-axis):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/c1e5fea69b62b94b565607da8d372160cc51dc127973f2afa4dda8cfc01116ea.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/7eff2673c3d979c233082bd82d26977e80eec3963f1ff142a4f3a7c63ef89790.png) the region can be expressed as \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/e20685d2d0ab9822cb380f6e5ddf2cf41a24bcc1d5c28b903b77b1c660e347cd.png)\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/92792892be94c40c231d24839bf521ea22dce3d4fd809cfc46b7d4312ebd5d4d.png)" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128951 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta$." 13194139535125 false] [17592186128951 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128951 1145 "First, we integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\int _{0}^{\\sin 2\\theta }rdrd\\theta  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}[r^{2}]_{0}^{\\sin 2\\theta }d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sin^{2} 2\\theta d\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sin^{2} 2\\theta d\\theta  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1 - \\cos 4\\theta )d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /8}1 - \\cos 4\\theta d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[\\theta  - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin 4\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8} - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) - (0 - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (0))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8} - \\frac{2}{8}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32}(\\pi  - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 false] [17592186128951 1145 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{[{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{2}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(\\pi -2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128952 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128952 1100 "The region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2030/Source/Images/e61cb3c40b449c031c9d9074f8983b94.png)" 13194139535125 false] [17592186128952 1145 "The diagram shows the first-quadrant quarter-disk of radius $1$, so $0\\le r\\le1$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$. Substitute $y=r\\sin\\theta$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$, and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$. Thus $$\\iint_Dy\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,dA=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^1(r\\sin\\theta)r\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^1r^3\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$" 13194139535125 false] [17592186128952 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128953 1100 "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0$." 13194139535125 false] [17592186128953 1100 "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0.$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128953 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({2}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32({sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186128953 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2030/Source/Images/3a076157edac08be902902303455d72d.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\iint _{D}15xy^{2}dA &= \\iint _{\\Delta }15r\\cos \\theta  \\cdot (r\\sin \\theta )^{2}rdrd\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}15r^{4}\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta drd\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta \\int _{0}^{2}5r^{4}drd\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta [r^{5}]_{0}^{2}d\\theta  \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta (2^{5} - 0)d\\theta  \\\\\n&= 32\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\cos \\theta \\sin^{2} \\theta d\\theta  \\\\\n&= 32[\\sin^{3} \\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\\n&= 32(\\sin^{3} (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) - \\sin^{3} (0)) \\\\\n&= 32(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 false] [17592186129133 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332460 13194139535125 true] [17592186129133 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332530 13194139535125 true] [17592186129133 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332544 13194139535125 true] [17592186129133 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332551 13194139535125 true] [17592186129134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332439 13194139535125 true] [17592186129134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332446 13194139535125 true] [17592186129134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332453 13194139535125 true] [17592186129134 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332509 13194139535125 true] [17592186129135 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332467 13194139535125 true] [17592186129135 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332474 13194139535125 true] [17592186129135 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332481 13194139535125 true] [17592186129135 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332488 13194139535125 true] [17592186129210 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332495 13194139535125 true] [17592186129210 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332502 13194139535125 true] [17592186129210 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332516 13194139535125 true] [17592186129210 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332523 13194139535125 true] [17592186129210 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332537 13194139535125 true] [17592186129565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535125 false] [17592186129565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535125 true] [17592186129565 1100 "Evaluate\n\n$\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{\\sin \\theta }2r\\cos \\theta drd\\theta $." 13194139535125 false] [17592186129565 1100 "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186129565 1145 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,[{r}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186129565 1145 "Integrate first with respect to $r$: $$\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^{\\sin\\theta}2r\\cos\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^2\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta.$$ Let $u=\\sin\\theta$, so $du=\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta$ and the bounds become $0,1$. The result is $\\int_0^1u^2\\,du=1/3$." 13194139535125 false] [17592186129566 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535125 true] [17592186129566 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2030/Source/Images/q-38076.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$\\iint _{D}2xydA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535125 false] [17592186129566 1145 "The diagram is the right half of the radius-$2$ disk: $0\\le r\\le2$ and $-\\pi/2\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$. Also $2xy=2r^2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta=r^2\\sin(2\\theta)$. Including the factor $r$ from $dA$ gives $$\\iint_D2xy\\,dA=\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^2r^3\\sin(2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$" 13194139535125 false] [17592186129566 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186129571 1100 "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 2x + y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y \\ge 0$." 13194139535125 false] [17592186129571 1100 "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186129571 1145 "The upper half-disk has $0\\le r\\le2$, $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$. Hence $$\\iint_D(2x+y)\\,dA=\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^2r^2(2\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\frac83\\int_0^\\pi(2\\cos\\theta+\\sin\\theta)\\,d\\theta.$$ The cosine integral is zero and the sine integral is $2$, giving $16/3$." 13194139535125 false] [17592186129571 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332439 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332440 13194139535125 true] [17592186332439 1089 "q-161038" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332439 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535125 true] [17592186332439 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/aa5b04b98d3796656fca912d8dc1732ea7e12bab9083f8ca543ad5fd4fdb10ff.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332439 1113 #uuid "c4ecad92-acdc-5cf3-b5ef-deae6c93b7ea" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332439 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332441 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332442 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332443 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332444 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332445 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1041 "selection" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332441 13194139535125 true] [17592186332440 1110 #uuid "c68cda96-6317-5f81-a372-f2ee047eeb93" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332441 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332441 1111 #uuid "9f3955b2-625b-57f0-b20c-bc953c43a2f2" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332442 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}{e}^{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332442 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332442 1111 #uuid "811d5009-2d2f-55b8-9a50-5ce9b8aa13ca" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332443 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332443 1111 #uuid "316791af-cd40-540b-9e7f-3a17e2b25356" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332444 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332444 1111 #uuid "c53fcf48-2fc5-5cee-8a93-694b035005aa" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332445 1034 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332445 1111 #uuid "2637541b-aea9-5097-ba6a-028850d2674f" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332446 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332447 13194139535125 true] [17592186332446 1089 "q-161039" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332446 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535125 true] [17592186332446 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/cdd5f0c58228b47a0bbba77daf2b426ce0247e71259e2b5af1a4a80fa833a036.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332446 1113 #uuid "e66da7ee-5802-527e-9784-38f625ec57f5" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332446 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332448 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332449 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332450 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332451 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332452 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1041 "selection" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332450 13194139535125 true] [17592186332447 1110 #uuid "50958a1a-ee2a-5b7d-81cf-65d19fadecd6" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332448 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332448 1111 #uuid "a5e0b8c3-b767-5ddd-8802-bdb562e912f1" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332449 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332449 1111 #uuid "d4aad787-b39d-5ca2-b6bb-221fdc827838" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332450 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332450 1111 #uuid "609b90ed-3121-5751-a1c9-26da88e3feb8" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332451 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int 