\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332483 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332484 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332485 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332486 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332487 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1041 "selection" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332484 13194139535125 true] [17592186332482 1110 #uuid "40ef08fa-60ea-5a02-ab50-7f90ab741943" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332483 1034 "9\\pi " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332483 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332483 1111 #uuid "94287c6e-5f22-59ba-939c-a5439cb4048d" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332484 1034 "81\\pi " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332484 1111 #uuid "139dc11b-3cb7-5f75-a8d6-a83f49a88e39" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332485 1034 "18\\pi +2" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332485 1111 #uuid "b7501a2f-cd2e-5205-a315-fc3d65415a55" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332486 1034 "27\\pi " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332486 1111 #uuid "55ea357a-637c-56f7-8326-50029fc1f454" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332487 1034 "9\\pi -3" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332487 1111 #uuid "b4b89e1b-8bf8-5290-b472-f2ee3755eec2" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332489 13194139535125 true] [17592186332488 1089 "q-161055" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332488 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535125 true] [17592186332488 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." 13194139535125 true] [17592186332488 1113 #uuid "06e5e252-a3f4-5add-b589-60c1a81d21b1" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332488 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332490 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332491 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332492 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332493 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332494 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1041 "selection" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332492 13194139535125 true] [17592186332489 1110 #uuid "eda02526-a3f5-5c6a-a71f-6207f3616233" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332490 1034 "2" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332490 1111 #uuid "01d133f2-26de-58da-ba8a-53d3dd75dd9f" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332491 1034 "\\pi " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332491 1111 #uuid "d82e343e-9dc8-5f46-baae-940630014471" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332492 1034 "1" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332492 1111 #uuid "9ce93789-5f77-5945-ab9f-f4e00ad6ffd8" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332493 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332493 1111 #uuid "eff0d9f0-e8fc-58ae-bf00-fd167038818c" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332494 1034 "0" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332494 1111 #uuid "0cefc46c-2881-5719-b445-ea73ebcb8faa" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332495 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332496 13194139535125 true] [17592186332495 1089 "q-161067" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332495 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535125 true] [17592186332495 1100 "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},$ then ${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A=$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332495 1113 #uuid "27f89428-72cb-554a-ae1c-3b9c76fc7457" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332495 1145 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/86036fd86096355b969da695f812c9bf835880edd68f7991183b70a6234e3eb4.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/0cf12dd41cddb943fe8b0950cdf13a0480cfbf2620a59d8bc4e8ba8775e0e6c1.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/cf2f0a011734a56f59cce2164a82d745c84b28d1bf706531deb80bb6e0a32640.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332497 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332498 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332499 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332500 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332501 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1041 "selection" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332497 13194139535125 true] [17592186332496 1110 #uuid "91e3b0ea-da7f-59db-a664-d8845c12044c" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332497 1034 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332497 1111 #uuid "2e202ec4-69a6-5c64-967e-f7b790b69117" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332498 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332498 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332498 1111 #uuid "f5861a77-b52d-5232-820c-f6feac1f1a7e" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332499 1034 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332499 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332499 1111 #uuid "708f0b62-e1fb-5d2d-90d1-7dd7624fea8e" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332500 1034 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332500 1111 #uuid "3d40782e-845e-5270-a0b3-32751174dde1" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332501 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535125 true] [17592186332501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535125 true] [17592186332501 1111 #uuid "c285bf83-963f-58b2-98c4-a67cad83c6c3" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332502 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332503 13194139535125 true] [17592186332502 1089 "q-161070" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332502 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535125 true] [17592186332502 1100 "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,y\\le 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=$" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332502 1113 #uuid "15d5d84c-a45c-5cae-a9ee-05c1c4659504" 13194139535125 true] [17592186332502 1145 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/d882bf70ecb7b94eee0eeeffbf63874ca8607268167f96278deb4678ec3b827a.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/55c9ea38874993dd10f03061fc3739ec6d7cb8af5b0467d5060a74a5ec22393a.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/b845816016bbc646e88af59bd02602b2e1127edfbc22ca82d1fb4e52cc63b41a.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\